/** @page developer-guide Developer guide

@section layout Layout

Public headers live under `include/atpg/`, one subdirectory per module, and
implementations mirror that structure under `src/`. Everything is one static
library, `atpg-core`, and one namespace, `atpg`, split into sub-namespaces by
responsibility rather than into separate CMake targets. The `atpg` executable
is a thin CLI over that library.

Tests live in `tests/unit/`, mirroring `src/`. Small hand-verifiable fixtures
live in `tests/data/`.

@section errors Error handling

atpg does not use exceptions. Every fallible function returns
@ref atpg::Result "Result<T>" - a value or an error message - or
@ref atpg::Status, for operations with no value. Both are `[[nodiscard]]`, and
failures propagate with the `ATPG_RETURN_IF_ERROR` and `ATPG_ASSIGN_OR_RETURN`
macros rather than hand-rolled checks.

The one unavoidable exception boundary is CLI11's `App::parse()`, which has no
non-throwing entry point. It is caught immediately at that boundary and turned
into an exit code; no exception reaches atpg's own code.

@section verification Verification

Several of this project's algorithms are ones where hand-derived expected
values stop being trustworthy beyond a single small case. For those, the tests
pair the usual hand-traced fixtures with a property test checking an invariant
against an independently derived ground truth over many generated circuits.

Two rules make those property tests worth their runtime:

- **The oracle is a second implementation.** `tests/unit/RandomCircuit.hpp`
  contains its own gate-level simulator, deliberately never delegating to
  @ref atpg::sim. A bug present in both the production simulator and the oracle
  would be invisible, and this project has twice found the same defect
  independently reintroduced in separately written modules.

- **Invariants must be able to fail.** Checking that an output is *sound* is
  not enough when a do-nothing implementation is also sound. Fault dropping and
  pattern compaction both need a *completeness* invariant as well - that no
  work was skipped - because outcome agreement alone is satisfied by a loop
  that drops nothing and a compactor that keeps everything. Each such invariant
  is verified by deliberately breaking the code and confirming the test fails
  and names itself.

@section conventions Conventions

- Format with clang-format, LLVM style, 100 columns; `.pre-commit-config.yaml`
  enforces it. Run `pre-commit install` once per clone.
- Use `fmt::format`/`fmt::print`, never stream operators or string
  concatenation. Catch2's `INFO`/`FAIL` macros are the one exception, since
  they require `<<` by design.
- Brace every control-flow body, including single-line ones.
- Parse command-line arguments with CLI11, not a hand-rolled argv loop.
- Document public functions, methods and classes with a `///` comment.

@section ortools A note on mixing Catch2 with OR-Tools

OR-Tools transitively pulls in abseil's fatal `CHECK`, which shadows Catch2's
`CHECK` and silently turns assertions into aborts. A test file that includes
OR-Tools headers directly must include them first, then `#undef CHECK`, then
Catch2's header, and only then `../Test.hpp` - which transitively includes
Catch2 and would otherwise satisfy its include guard before the `#undef` runs.
See `tests/unit/gen/TestGenTests.cpp`.

*/
